  "id": "Compound::DB01215 - Gene::2568",
  "verbalization": "Estazolam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrr2, Gabrd, and Gabrg3, all of which are subunits of the Gaba-a receptor. These receptors are critical for the modulation of neuronal activity, and their participation in Gaba-a receptor activity is directly linked to Gabrp, a protein that plays a role in the structural and functional integrity of the receptor complex. The consistent binding of Estazolam to these related Gaba-a receptor subunits suggests a potential mechanism by which the drug may influence Gaba-a receptor function, thereby affecting neuronal signaling. The evidence from the paths highlights a recurring pattern of Estazolam interacting with Gaba-a receptor subunits, which are all involved in the same biological process, reinforcing the likelihood of a mechanistic link between Estazolam and Gabrp. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Estazolam may modulate Gaba-a receptor activity through interaction with these subunits, potentially influencing synaptic transmission and neuronal function.",
